#
# ROSTER RECONCILIATION - the half of the old spawn_players that needs nothing from the
# map: cull the roster to PLAYER_COUNT, apply a game code's loadout hull/name, rebuild
# stats, hand the server its ship.
#
# server_console's `start` fires this BEFORE it schedules the map, which is the whole
# point. A console only ever needed one thing to be displayable - a valid player ship -
# and it has had one since the server console opened. Running this first means the ship
# it attaches to is the ship it KEEPS: already culled, already carrying the coded hull.
# Without that, a crew with a game-code loadout would watch their hull and stats change
# underneath a live console.
#
# A //shared/signal route with no await runs synchronously inside signal_emit (the same
# property create_sides relies on), so the roster is final by the time `start` returns
# from the emit - no flag, no poll, no wait.
#
# //shared so it runs ONCE on the server, not once per connected console.
#
# Guarded on PLAYER_CREATE_DEFAULT: if a mission did not let LM build the roster, LM has
# no business culling it. a2x-converted missions build players on their own side and turn
# the flag off (arme2cosmos convert.py), and PLAYER_COUNT means nothing to them.
#
# Idempotent by IDENTITY rather than by a latch: the list is default_player_ship AND
# __player__, and culling strips __player__ - so an already-culled hull drops out of the
# list and a second emit is a no-op instead of a double delete.
#
//shared/signal/reconcile_player_roster if PLAYER_CREATE_DEFAULT
    #
    # Latched, so emitting this more than once is free. It is emitted from two places on
    # purpose - `start`, early, and spawn_players, as a backstop for any path that starts
    # a map without going through `start` - and re-running the body would re-apply
    # setup_from_data, wiping anything a map had set on a ship in between.
    #
    # `players_cleaned` IS the latch: create_default_player_ships clears it whenever it
    # builds a roster, so a rebuild after a sim wipe re-arms this without a special case.
    #
    default shared players_cleaned = False
    ->END if players_cleaned
    #
    # THE ROSTER OWNS THE RESHAPE. This used to walk the live `__player__` list, indexed by
    # position, culling and re-hulling in one loop. Each step is now a named roster call, and
    # the roster is keyed by SLOT - so a console that binds to a slot rather than to an
    # object survives everything below.
    #
    # Re-seeded rather than assumed: `start` is not the only path in, and a sim wipe can have
    # rebuilt the ships since the records were made. Seeding is idempotent by slot and leaves
    # active-ness and console bindings alone.
    #
    player_roster_seed(SETTINGS.get("PLAYER_LIST"))
    # Park everything past PLAYER_COUNT. Parking is the same de-roled, invisible, side-less
    # state as before; what is new is that the ship still EXISTS and keeps its slot marker,
    # so nothing is holding a pointer to something that is about to be freed.
    player_roster_set_count(PLAYER_COUNT)
    # loadout is [] unless a game code carried a SHIP_LOADOUT, so this is a no-op for maps
    # that don't use one. It WINS over a theater - the crew chose those hulls explicitly.
    loadout = player_loadout_active()
    # force=True keeps this point's old behavior exactly: every surviving ship gets its stats
    # rebuilt "in case art work changed", not just the ones whose hull moved. The panel path
    # calls apply() WITHOUT force, where the diff is what makes a repaint free.
    player_roster_apply(loadout, force=True)
    # Deferred by a tick, as it always was: delete_object frees the C++ object synchronously.
    # What is new is that release refuses a slot a console is still crewing.
    task_schedule(player_ships_remove_unused)
    # The server console rides slot 0.
    _first = player_roster_resolve(0)
    if _first is not None:
        assign_client_to_ship(0, _first)

    # "the roster is final". Nothing waits on it any more - it is kept because missions
    # ask the question, and select_console reads it to sync PLAYER_COUNT.
    shared players_cleaned = True
    ->END


====== spawn_players =======
    #
    # PLACEMENT. Despite the name this has not spawned anything for a long time, and it
    # no longer culls either - that moved to //shared/signal/reconcile_player_roster
    # above, which runs before the map is even scheduled. Every ship reaching this loop
    # is one that is staying.
    #
    # What is left genuinely needs the map: put the players near a friendly station and
    # link them to friendly eyes. Only the map knows where those are, which is why this
    # stays a label a map calls rather than becoming a route - a map that wants its own
    # placement positions its ships after calling this (deepstrike, OU do exactly that),
    # or never calls it at all.
    #
    # The name stays as it is. It is public surface: at least seven missions outside LM
    # call it by name, the ai_scripting guide documents it as the fleets addon's entry
    # point, and arme2cosmos has a test asserting converted missions do NOT call it.
    #
    # BACKSTOP. `start` normally reconciles the roster before the map is scheduled, but
    # not every path goes through `start` - cosmos_dev's `--map` schedules a map label
    # directly, and so can a mission that drives its own map. Calling spawn_players has
    # always been enough to get a culled roster and it stays enough. The route is
    # latched, so on the normal path this emit is free.
    signal_emit("reconcile_player_roster")
    spawn_pos = Vec3(0,0,0)
    # Sorted by id, not raw set order: placement draws from the RNG per ship, so a stable
    # order is what keeps a seeded map reproducible.
    player_ships = sorted(to_object_list(role("default_player_ship") & role("__player__")), key=lambda s: s.id)
    for player_ship in player_ships:
        side = player_ship.side
        stations_friendly = to_object_list(side_members_set(side)  & role("station"))

        if len(stations_friendly) > 0:
            so = random.choice(stations_friendly)
            spawn_pos = so.pos
            spawn_pos = spawn_pos.rand_offset(3500, 5300, ring=True)
            player_ship.pos = spawn_pos

        #
        # schedule and set friendly eyes
        # link or unlink extra_scan_source
        #
        friendly_eyes = side_ally_members_set(player_ship)  & (role("station")|role("defender"))
        link(player_ship, "extra_scan_source", friendly_eyes)

        #player_ship_update_friendly(player_ship, stations_friendly, True)

    #
    # One yield, here rather than nowhere. A map body is otherwise entirely synchronous,
    # so without a break somewhere the whole opening - stations, NPCs, terrain, fleets -
    # lands in a single frame. Breaking after placement splits it where it costs least.
    #
    await delay_sim(0)
    ->END

==== default_player_friendly_eyes
" Schedule this label if all player ships are on the same side and should share scan data.  
" If `players` isn't specified in the metadata, then it will use `role('__player__')`. 
metadata: ```
players:
```
    if players is None:
        players = role('__player__')
    for p in players:
        for o in players:
            if p != o:
                link(p, "extra_scan_source", o)
    ->END
    
==== player_ships_remove_unused
    # Kept by NAME - it is scheduled from the reconcile route above and the name has been
    # public surface for a long time - but it no longer takes a ship id. The roster decides
    # WHICH slots are releasable, and refuses any a console is still bound to; a bare
    # delete_object on an id handed over a tick earlier is precisely what could not make
    # that check.
    player_roster_release_inactive()
    ->END

        
